## What the existing cause view actually does

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The majority group owns the original ticket in this view; the minority has no ticket of its
own in the export. This view and the report table above use the same grouping.
The ticket-search links above locate the work items by title,
without assuming future GitHub issue numbers. After publication, verify that each search finds
the intended issue.
Desk-noted IDs stay counted once in parser scope, with their observations and limits
shown above. This follows the exported counting convention, not proof that each body has a
demonstrated parser root cause. A future independent problem needs evidence of its own; the
note list does not establish an additional cause. Do not infer a confirmed timezone defect from the wording.

## How the queue hid this

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The received and merged series measure different events. A merge spike can reflect processing
older reports; it does not establish when the bug began. Read the received series to track
incoming reports. “Known” means the desk recognizes a problem, not that it is fixed or that new
reports can be ignored. Compare the review metadata with the reporting period shown by the components.

## Why subject-only deduplication fails

<DedupeExplorer />

The accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) and
[triage text](/kb/triage/dedupe) prescribe matching normalized subjects. Their review dates are
stale under the KB's own review policy. The explorer actually compares causes using tenant and
channel: identical subjects can need different fixes, and different subjects can need one fix.
Changing punctuation cannot fix that mismatch between the written rule and its working example.
The explorer itself has no report-body or report-ID input. It demonstrates formatting routes;
it cannot establish that a temporal observation has the same cause as a parser report.

For this export, a booking-v3 form at Alder Physio differs from an app report at the same tenant;
form reports at Copper Studio and Morrow Tax also differ because the form versions differ.
Unknown tenants are not evidence of a shared cause and must stay unmerged pending investigation.

The support desk covers sched-prod and other products. Outside this date incident, do not reuse
this classifier as a universal merge rule. Read the report, identify the product and affected
workflow, obtain the configuration and reproduction evidence relevant to that product, and
merge only when evidence supports the same underlying problem and fix. Preserve report IDs,
counts and first-received dates. When evidence is missing, keep reports separate for triage.

## Next actions and verification

- Work the linked mechanisms separately; each issue has its own narrowly defined fix and checks.
- Preserve and review desk-noted observations without counting their IDs a second time or treating
  a footnote as proof of an additional cause.
- Review [proposed decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-evidence) with the desk owner.
- Before live triage, compare a fresh export with this snapshot by report ID, recheck tenant
  settings and add new reports without counting repeated export rows twice.
- After fixes, test both an ambiguous date and a date whose day is greater than twelve through
  each affected route. Check what the form says, what is stored and what the invite shows.
- Have the owner update operational triage guidance after approving the replacement decision.
  Decision 0001 is preserved unchanged as the accepted historical record.

